  {
   "title": "How Filipinos eat",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Filipinos love food, and it is a big part of their famous hospitality. Foreign guests often say they were overfed when visiting Filipino homes."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Five eating times a day",
     "items": [
      "**Three full meals**, plus a **morning snack** and an **afternoon snack**.",
      "**Cooked rice** is essential at the meals, with a dish of meat and/or vegetables.",
      "The snacks are called **merienda**, and they often include rice in its sweet, glutinous form."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Where to eat",
     "t": "Simple roadside eateries are called **karinderia** or **turo-turò**. Turo-turò gets its name because customers point to (**turò**) the food they want to order."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Whether at a roadside eatery, a restaurant or at home, some dishes cross every regional and social border and appear on every Filipino table. They follow below."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "eat",
