  {
   "title": "Popular dishes: adóbo to karé-karé",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Dish",
      "What it is"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[Adóbo|noun]]",
       "Seen by many as the official national dish: pork or poultry (or both) cooked in vinegar with garlic, peppercorns, soy sauce and bayleaf."
      ],
      [
       "[[Arroz cáldo|noun]]",
       "Rice-based gruel with bits of chicken and ginger; a favorite merienda, best steaming hot."
      ],
      [
       "[[Balút|noun]]",
       "Street food: a steamed fertilized 17-day old duck embryo, eaten with salt or vinegar."
      ],
      [
       "[[Bibíngka|noun]]",
       "Glutinous rice cake with toppings such as cheese, salted egg or grated coconut and sugar."
      ],
      [
       "[[puto|noun]]",
       "A small plain rice cake with just cheese or no topping."
      ],
      [
       "[[kakanín|noun]]",
       "Similar preparations from cassava (kamóteng káhoy) and glutinous rice with coconut milk (gatâ)."
      ],
      [
       "[[Dinuguán|noun]]",
       "Pig's blood stew with cut-ups of pork and pig organs, in a dark broth of vinegar and pig's blood; paired with puto."
      ],
      [
       "[[Ginataán|noun]]",
       "Traditional merienda: sweet potato, nangkâ, sabá, glutinous rice balls and tapioca pearls (sagó) in slightly thick coconut milk."
      ],
      [
       "[[Hálo-halò|noun]]",
       "Literally *mix-mix*: sweetened root tubers and fruits (sweet potato, sabá, nangkâ, garbanzos, ube, yam, red beans) topped with shaved ice and evaporated milk."
      ],
      [
       "[[Karé-karé|noun]]",
       "Oxtail stew: pork or calf leg and oxtail with egg plant, banana heart, string beans and pechay (bok choi) in peanut sauce with a dash of bagoóng (shrimp paste)."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Dishes and delicacies (1)"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "dish1",
     "title": "Dishes: adóbo to karé-karé"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "ingr",
     "title": "Ingredients named in the dishes"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Popular dishes: lechón to sinigáng",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Dish",
      "What it is"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[Lechón|noun]] / [[litsón|noun]]",
       "Roasted suckling pig, cooked over a low charcoal fire for a day; crisp red skin and succulent meat."
      ],
      [
       "[[Lumpiá|noun]]",
       "Fried spring roll of sauteed vegetables and ground meat in a thin egg or flour wrapper, topped with peanut sauce and crisp ginger bits."
      ],
      [
       "[[saríwang lumpiá|noun]]",
       "Fresh lumpiá: the other spring roll, without the wrapper."
      ],
      [
       "[[Menúdo|noun]]",
       "Diced pork and liver cooked with potato, garbanzos and tomato sauce or fresh tomatoes."
      ],
      [
       "[[Paksíw|noun]]",
       "Fish or meat cooked with enough vinegar, ginger and other spices, usually bayleaf and pepper."
      ],
      [
       "[[Pinakbét|noun]]",
       "Sauteed native vegetables with bagoóng or strips of dried fish."
      ],
      [
       "[[Sinigáng|noun]]",
       "Sour broth of shrimp, fish or pork with vegetables (okra, kangkong and string beans), made sour by tamarind or kamiás (green sour fruit)."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Dishes and delicacies (2)"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Lechón, island by island",
     "items": [
      "The name **lechón** comes from the Spanish **leche** (milk); it is also spelled **litsón**.",
      "The slow roasting gives crisp red skin and succulent meat.",
      "A lechón from **Luzon** is usually served with **liver sauce**.",
      "A lechón from the **Visayas and Mindanao** is stuffed with spices, wines and even **paélla** (a Spanish dish of meat and seafood), and needs no sauce."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "dish2",
     "title": "Dishes: lechón to sinigáng"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Cooking verbs: ihurnó (bake), pakuluín (boil), iháwin (broil), pritúhin (fry), igisá (sauté), ibabad (soak), tustahín (toast).",
      "Cut with a knife = hiwáin; cut with scissors = gupitín.",
      "Filipinos eat three meals plus two snacks (merienda) a day, with rice at the center.",
      "Adóbo is seen as the national dish; lechón (litsón) is roasted suckling pig.",
      "Hálo-halò literally means mix-mix."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
